{
  "id": "14-test-the-voice",
  "title": "Test the Voice",
  "tenet": "Listen with courage and discernment",
  "durationMs": 72552,
  "audioAsset": "kingdom/14-test-the-voice.mp3",
  "transcriptAsset": "assets/kingdom/14-test-the-voice.json",
  "text": "Can a strong feeling be sincere and still need testing?\n\nA woman felt certain she should confront a friend that evening. Her words sounded brave in her mind, but she also wanted to win an old argument. She paused, prayed, and wrote down what she hoped the conversation would produce. After listening to the friend the next day, she still spoke the truth, but without the accusation she had rehearsed. The good impulse survived being examined. Her pride did not need to lead it.\n\nPeter received a vision that challenged what he thought he knew. He was in doubt about its meaning and pondered it. The message came again, and events helped him understand that God was welcoming people he had kept at a distance. His honest question was not rebellion. Scripture tells us not to quench the Spirit, and also to test everything. Confidence or a first thought is not proof of God's voice; doubt is not automatically fear or sin. Ask whether an impression agrees with Scripture, grows love, respects safety, and bears good fruit. Seek wise counsel when others may be affected. If clarity grows, take the faithful step. Keep a daily listening habit, and remain willing for God to correct your understanding.",
